  "narrative_body": "At 3:49 on the morning of May 15, Finland's Interior Ministry issued an emergency warning across Uusimaa — the greater Helsinki region, home to 1.8 million people — after intelligence suggested Ukrainian drones carrying heavy explosive payloads might stray into Finnish airspace. It was the clearest demonstration yet that the operational effects of the Ukraine-Russia war reach into NATO's northernmost capital.\n\nHelsinki-Vantaa airport was closed. Antti Häkkänen, the defence minister, authorized use of force. Petteri Orpo, the prime minister, was recalled from his cottage. By 7:06 AM the all-clear was issued, and Timo Herranen, Finland's air force commander, confirmed at a press conference that no drone had entered Finnish territory. The aftermath brought a complicating detail: the Finnish Defence Forces later confirmed the drones had approached from the Russian direction, not from Ukrainian airspace — meaning they had already passed through or over Russian territory before nearing the Finnish border, a finding that leaves the full incident geometry unresolved.\n\nFinland has now twice formally asserted airspace sovereignty against Ukrainian drone spillover. Alexander Stubb, the president, called Volodymyr Zelensky overnight. Mr Häkkänen stated publicly that if Ukrainian drone operations endanger NATO member states, \"it is condemnable and wrong every time.\" The dual-track Ukraine relationship — deepen the defence partnership, enforce sovereignty — has moved from posture to operational fact.\n\nThe incident also exposed a gap in Finland's civil protection system. The rescue chief initially told reporters that at least one drone may have entered Finnish territory, then retracted the claim as a miscommunication. Citizens had no SMS emergency alert — Finland's EU-alert system will not be available until 2027 — and were reportedly uncertain whether to send children to school. Mr Orpo acknowledged the failure: \"People need to have clear information about how to act when a warning is given — can you drop the children at daycare, can you go to work in elderly care. We need to take this in hand.\" A parliamentary defence committee hearing was convened with Mr Häkkänen and Mari Rantanen, the interior minister; Mr Orpo committed to urgent SMS alert deployment.\n\nThe drone crisis unfolded against a week in which Mr Stubb was already staking out new ground on Russia. In interviews with Italian outlet Corriere della Sera, Lithuanian media, and MTV, he said \"now is the time to start talking with Russia,\" conditioning the argument on Ukraine's current battlefield strength, and urged Europe to \"calm down\" over US troop withdrawals from Germany. Mr Häkkänen countered that Russia's continued full wartime mobilisation was not the posture of a country seeking peace. Mr Orpo mentioned only \"weak signals\" of Russian interest; Elina Valtonen, the foreign minister, assessed separately that Vladimir Putin may have genuine interest in talks. Whether this reflects coordinated messaging or a real divergence on strategic timing between the president and the defence establishment is the week's open question.\n\nMr Stubb had a crowded week beyond Helsinki. He attended the Bucharest Nine summit in Romania as an invited Nordic guest — Finland is not a formal member — where he met Zelensky and Mark Rutte, the NATO secretary general, and pressed for stronger alliance unity. Reports at the summit suggest the Nordics may eventually be formally integrated into the B9 format. He also conducted a state visit to Lithuania, attended the Europe Gulf Forum in Greece, and held bilateral talks with Mark Carney, the Canadian prime minister.\n\nMeanwhile, all five opposition parties filed an interpellation — the 14th against the Orpo government — forcing a parliamentary reckoning on economic policy. Mr Orpo acknowledged on the floor that the government's debt-to-GDP stabilisation target \"appears unlikely to be achieved,\" blaming external shocks: US tariffs, threats to Greenland, the Iran war's economic effects. The government survived the subsequent no-confidence vote; coalition discipline held. But the admission marks a break. For three years the government had attributed poor performance to external factors while maintaining its target commitments. That defence has now been publicly abandoned. The Finance Ministry projects debt reaching 99% of GDP by 2030. Riikka Purra, the finance minister, maintained the consolidation course and attributed rising poverty almost entirely to immigration; Tytti Tuppurainen, who leads the SDP parliamentary group, called it \"Europe's worst economic management.\"\n\nAn academic survey published this week set the public backdrop in sharper relief. Some 73% of Finns would still vote for NATO membership and 80% say it has increased their security — but trust in the United States has effectively collapsed. About 80% say they cannot trust Washington to defend European allies, 80% want to reduce dependence on US weapons and technology, and only one-quarter trust the bilateral defence cooperation agreement. Only one-third now see NATO as a liberal democratic community, down from nearly half a year ago. The researchers described their findings as \"tense\" with government messaging. With Finland locked into the F-35 for 40 years, the public has recognised a dependency the government has no near-term way to dissolve. Karelian Sword 26 — 10,000 troops, with US and UK participation — begins May 18.",
  "other_stories": [
    {
      "headline": "PM Orpo's Eurovision flamethrower videos go viral, drawing praise and criticism at home and abroad",
      "summary": "PM Orpo posted a series of Instagram videos wielding a flamethrower to support Finland's Eurovision entry Liekinheitin ('Flamethrower') ahead of and after the Vienna final. The videos triggered sharply divided social media reaction in Finland, extensive domestic media coverage of the controversy, and Swedish tabloid Aftonbladet wrote that Orpo had 'jinxed' Finland's chances after Finland finished sixth (behind winner Bulgaria). Post-final, Orpo invited the Finnish duo to his official summer residence Kesäranta."
    },
    {
      "headline": "SDP MP Joona Räsänen admits April drink-driving offence, steps back from party positions",
      "summary": "SDP MP Joona Räsänen publicly admitted he was stopped by police for drink-driving in Espoo in April and described it as a serious error in judgement. He said he would not seek re-election to the SDP party executive board or the Uusimaa district chairmanship. SDP leader Lindtman and parliamentary group chair Tuppurainen both condemned his conduct, stating it was up to voters to judge him as an MP."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Finnish financial regulator fines Oma Säästöpankki €400,000 for insider register violations",
      "summary": "The Finnish Financial Supervisory Authority (Fiva) imposed a €400,000 penalty on Oma Säästöpankki for failing to maintain insider registers in two separate incidents in 2021 and 2022. The cases concerned a software contract termination with Cognizant and a second undisclosed matter. The bank's cooperation with Fiva reduced the fine; Fiva had previously made a police referral regarding the same bank in 2024."
    }
  ]
}

